VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: S5
ATTEMPT: 4
---
Attacked the "scan the Makefile's own text for `$(GO) test`" guard from
.swarm/manifests/S5.4.files (merged wt-primary, per .swarm/tier3/S5/report.md
RESOLUTION). Found a reproducible silent-evasion class that is the same
failure category attempt 2 was FAILed for, and worse: it is open-ended, not
a fixed missed target.

REPRODUCTION (in a scratch copy of the real repo, node stripped from PATH):

  cd <scratch-copy-of-repo>
  cat >> Makefile <<'MK'
  GOCMD := $(GO)
  sneaky-alias: check-go
  	$(GOCMD) test ./internal/handlers/accounts/...
  MK
  env PATH=/usr/bin:/bin make sneaky-alias
  =>
  /home/darrell/.local/go/bin/go test ./internal/handlers/accounts/...
  ok  	budget2/internal/handlers/accounts	0.224s

No "ERROR: node is required" line, no check-node invocation, exit 0. The
harness-carrying test (TestSyncWarnings_ClientRegressionHarness) silently
t.Skip()s because node is absent, and the target goes green — exactly the
"go test ./... test-unit went green with the harness silently skipped"
defect that failed attempt 2, now reachable through any target that spells
the invocation as anything other than the literal adjacent tokens "$(GO)"
+ " test" on one line. Also reproduced with:
  - a `define RUN_GO_TEST ... $(GO) test ... endef` canned recipe invoked as
    `$(RUN_GO_TEST)` (real Make idiom for DRY test targets) — silently
    unguarded.
  - a line-continuation split (`$(GO) \` newline `test ./...`) — silently
    unguarded.
  - bare `go test ./...` with no $(GO) at all — silently unguarded (unless
    accidentally rescued by the misattribution bug below).
This is not exotic: the Makefile itself already uses variable indirection
for its tool path (GO_SYSTEM/GO_LOCAL -> GO), so `GOCMD := $(GO)` is a
completely ordinary pattern in this exact codebase's own style.

SECOND, INDEPENDENT BUG — misattribution across target boundaries. The awk
scanner is a single-pass state machine that remembers "current target = t"
from the last line matching /^[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+:/, and attributes ANY later
line containing the literal "$(GO) test" to that remembered t, regardless of
whether that line is actually inside t's own recipe. Appending
  define RUN_GO_TEST
  $(GO) test ./internal/...
  endef
  sneaky-canned: check-go
  	$(RUN_GO_TEST)
after the Makefile's real `release:` target causes `release` (last real
target header seen before the define block) to be incorrectly granted
check-node as a prerequisite, verified live:
  env PATH=/usr/bin:/bin make -n release v=1.2.3
  => first printed command is check-node's `command -v node ... || { echo
     ERROR: node is required ...; exit 1; }`
`make release` now fails on any machine without node, even though release
never runs go test — the exact "build/vet/static/dev/install must not
require node" regression class the brief calls out, just on a different
target (release) than the ones enumerated. Meanwhile `sneaky-canned`
(the actual go-test invocation) gets NO guard at all — the same bug
produces a false positive on an unrelated target and a false negative on
the real offender simultaneously.

THE ORACLE HAS THE SAME BLIND SPOT. accept.sh's own gotest_targets scan
(independent regex over `make -pn`'s database: `\tgo test|\t.*\$\(GO\)
test|\tgotestsum`) was checked against the same three sneaky targets in the
real repo's make -pn dump:
  sneaky-alias  recipe: `$(GOCMD) test ...`      -> no oracle regex match
  sneaky-canned2 recipe: `$(RUN_GO_TEST)`         -> no oracle regex match
  sneaky-bare   recipe: `go test ...`             -> matches `\tgo test`
Both the alias and canned-recipe evasions are invisible to the oracle too,
so re-running accept.sh against a Makefile carrying either would print
ORACLE: PASS — the escape is undetectable by the instrument the report
relies on to declare the winner robust. This directly contradicts the
report's stated reason for preferring wt-primary over wt-alt ("recipe-
content driven, so a target added later is covered without anyone
remembering to wire it") — that claim is false for two ordinary Make idioms
(variable aliasing, canned recipes), and the Makefile's own added comment
makes the same false promise to future maintainers.

Per the task brief: "Silent evaporation is a worse defect than the one this
task fixed and is a FAIL." That is what was found. Not a hypothetical edge
case — reproduced against the actual repo tree with node genuinely absent
from PATH, using idioms already present in this very Makefile's style.

OTHER ANGLES CHECKED, NO DEFECT FOUND:
- Self-reference trap: `grep -n '\$(GO) test' Makefile` in the real tree
  returns 0 matches — the scanner's own line does not self-tag.
- awk presence/portability: system awk resolves to mawk; GOTEST_TARGETS
  computed correctly (`fuzz race test test-coverage test-integration
  test-unit`), matches oracle's list.
- No over-application to build/clean/vet/static/dev in the unmodified tree:
  `make -n build|clean|vet|static|dev` with node stripped from PATH shows
  no check-node step for any of them.
- Guard fires loudly (nonzero make exit, ERROR line) for test, test-unit,
  test-integration, test-coverage, race in the unmodified tree with node
  absent — confirmed by direct (non -n) execution.
- PASS regex `(?m)^RESULT <name> PASS(\s|$)` in
  internal/handlers/accounts/warnings_client_regression_test.go:147 uses
  regexp.QuoteMeta(name) and requires PASS at start of a line followed by
  whitespace/EOL; not chased further given the decisive finding above.

Did not compare against wt-primary/wt-alt worktrees under
/tmp/swarm-worktrees (not reached; the defect above is dispositive on its
own and no repo files were modified — all mutation was in scratch copies
under /tmp/claude-1000/.../scratchpad).
